package greentic:provider-instance-identity@0.1.0;

/// Phase M1.4: per-endpoint instance discrimination.
///
/// Sibling contract to `greentic:provider-schema-core`. Provider components
/// export this in addition to `schema-core` when they host multiple
/// instances of the same `provider_type` on one runtime — e.g.
/// `teams-legal-bot` and `teams-accounting-bot` are both `provider_type =
/// "teams"` but are distinct identities discriminated by bot id.
///
/// The contract is opt-in. Providers that own a single instance per
/// environment (or whose inbound payloads carry no information that lets
/// them tell instances apart) SHOULD NOT export this world; the host
/// treats absence as "single-instance fallback" and uses the operator's
/// statically-declared `provider_id` without inspecting the payload.
///
/// Implementations MAY identify the instance from EITHER the inbound
/// payload (e.g. Slack `team_id`, Teams `bot_id`, Telegram bot-token
/// hash) OR pre-shared configuration values that were validated at boot.
///
/// Returning `none` means "this payload does not match a known instance
/// of this provider type". The host fold (see
/// `RunnerHost::identify_messaging_endpoints_for_revision`) decides
/// admission against the env's `(provider_type, provider_id) -> endpoint_id`
/// admit table:
///
/// - Env declares exactly ONE endpoint of this `provider_type` AND the
///   probe returns `none`: host falls through to the single-instance
///   fallback (admission proceeds against the operator's statically-
///   declared `provider_id`). Backward-compatible with single-instance
///   setups that have not yet provisioned a per-instance discriminator.
/// - Env declares two or more endpoints AND no probed component returns
///   `some(provider-id)` for any of them: resolver poisons to Ambiguous
///   and greentic-start rejects the request 422 (fail-closed; without a
///   discriminator we cannot route between siblings).
interface instance-identity-api {
  /// Inspect `input-json` and return the matching `provider_id` if any.
  /// The returned id MUST match the `provider_id` declared on a
  /// `MessagingEndpoint` registration in the operator's environment for the
  /// host to admit the request.
  ///
  /// # Payload shape (M1.4d wrapper)
  ///
  /// `input-json` is a UTF-8 JSON document. As of host wrapper-payload
  /// (M1.4d), the host SENDS a wrapper object:
  ///
  /// ```json
  /// {
  ///   "headers": [
  ///     { "name": "x-telegram-bot-api-secret-token", "value": "…" }
  ///   ],
  ///   "body": { /* the original parsed JSON request body */ }
  /// }
  /// ```
  ///
  /// The host whitelists routing-relevant headers (an explicit
  /// per-header allowlist — currently `x-telegram-bot-api-secret-token`
  /// only) so that providers whose discriminator does not live in the
  /// body (notably Telegram, whose payload does not carry the bot's
  /// identity) can identify the instance from the request headers.
  /// Bodies that don't parse as JSON are passed through as `body: null`.
  ///
  /// Implementations MUST accept BOTH the wrapper shape AND a bare
  /// inbound body at the top level — older hosts and direct test
  /// harnesses still pass raw bodies, and shipping a wrapper-only
  /// implementation would break backward compatibility with deployed
  /// hosts. The conventional implementation pattern is:
  /// 1. Try to extract the discriminator from the JSON at the top level.
  /// 2. If absent, fall back to extracting from `value["body"]` (the
  ///    wrapped body) and `value["headers"]` (the wrapped header list).
  ///
  /// This contract is non-authoritative: the discriminator is caller-
  /// controllable. Downstream auth (signature / JWT / shared-secret
  /// validation) MUST verify the routed-to endpoint's credentials before
  /// the host admits the request or emits any events.
  identify-instance: func(input-json: list<u8>) -> option<string>;
}

/// Phase D: optional metadata describing how a component identifies instances.
///
/// Separated into its own interface so that existing components that export
/// only `instance-identity-api` continue to instantiate without change.
/// Components that additionally export this interface let the host scope
/// per-provider header allowlists and potentially short-circuit simple
/// extraction without invoking the full `identify-instance` function.
///
/// Returns JSON-encoded `IdentifyInstanceHint`:
/// ```json
/// {
///   "version": 1,
///   "sources": [
///     { "header": { "name": "x-telegram-bot-api-secret-token" } },
///     { "body_path": { "json_pointer": "/recipient/id" } }
///   ]
/// }
/// ```
///
/// The hint MUST include a top-level `"version": 1` integer field.
/// Hosts MUST reject hints where `version` is missing or not equal to `1`
/// — treat the hint as malformed and fall back to unhinted behavior
/// (invoke `identify-instance` with the global header allowlist).
/// This forward-compatibility gate ensures that future shape extensions
/// (e.g. compound discriminators like `all_of`) are never silently
/// ignored by older hosts.
///
/// The hint is an OPTIONAL short-circuit. For compound discriminators
/// not expressible in the hint schema, or when the host is uncertain
/// about the hint's applicability, the host MUST invoke the full
/// `identify-instance` function rather than skipping it.
///
/// First match within `sources` wins. Components that do not export this
/// interface are treated as unhinted — the host falls back to invoking
/// `identify-instance` with all allowlisted inputs.
///
/// Return-value semantics: the function returns `none` when the component
/// cannot construct a hint (e.g. configuration-dependent and config not
/// yet loaded). The host treats `none` identically to a missing export:
/// unhinted, falls back to invoking `identify-instance` with the global
/// header allowlist. The function MUST NOT return `none` to signal "fail
/// closed" — that is the host's policy, not the component's. Components
/// that genuinely cannot serve hints SHOULD NOT export this interface at
/// all.
interface instance-identity-describe-api {
  /// Return an optional JSON-encoded hint describing how this component
  /// extracts the instance discriminator from inbound requests.
  ///
  /// Returns `none` when the component cannot construct a hint; the host
  /// treats this identically to a missing export (unhinted fallback).
  describe-identify-instance: func() -> option<list<u8>>;
}

world instance-identity {
  export instance-identity-api;
}

world instance-identity-describe {
  export instance-identity-api;
  export instance-identity-describe-api;
}
